+    What a point that passes the residual test is can be stated without a convergence theorem. Suppose the iteration has reached a fixed point: every agent's copy of every consensus variable equals the global or counterpart value, the duals no longer change, and each local solve of \eqref{eq:admm_network_local} and \eqref{eq:admm_esso_local} ends at a point satisfying the Karush--Kuhn--Tucker (KKT) conditions of its own augmented problem. At such a point the quadratic consensus terms and their gradients vanish, the linear dual terms are the Lagrangian terms of the coupling equalities with the ADMM duals as their multipliers, and the first-order conditions of the local problems add up, block by block, to the KKT conditions of the coupled recourse problem with its consensus constraints imposed exactly. A point that passes the residual test and the local-solve checks to the stated tolerances is therefore an approximate first-order stationary point of the coupled nonconvex recourse that satisfies the network equations of every block and the consensus constraints within those tolerances. No statement about global optimality follows, and none is made; the sequential nonconvex scheme used here is outside the convergence theory of consensus ADMM for convex or two-block nonconvex problems, and convergence is observed, not proved.
